1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,750
Imagine we had a technology that
could repair the human body.

2
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:06,230
We could remove damaged cells.

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,070
Replace worn-out tissue.

4
00:00:08,925 --> 00:00:10,575
Repair damaged DNA.

5
00:00:11,005 --> 00:00:12,835
Rejuvenate mitochondria.

6
00:00:13,245 --> 00:00:16,495
We could even make some cells
biologically young again.

7
00:00:17,236 --> 00:00:21,526
An eighty-year-old could have the
body of a forty-year-old. So...

8
00:00:21,816 --> 00:00:24,786
we've solved ageing.
Could we live forever?

9
00:00:25,707 --> 00:00:26,527
Maybe not.

10
00:00:26,607 --> 00:00:30,097
Because there is one part of the body
that makes this much more complicated.

11
00:00:30,727 --> 00:00:31,927
The brain.

12
00:00:32,246 --> 00:00:34,486
And one recent mathematical model took a

13
00:00:34,566 --> 00:00:37,896
perfectly maintained human
body and did the maths.

14
00:00:38,986 --> 00:00:42,416
It might eventually reach somewhere
around a hundred and fifty years.

15
00:00:43,224 --> 00:00:45,134
But is that really a hard limit?

16
00:00:45,864 --> 00:00:47,514
And why would the brain be the problem?

17
00:00:49,984 --> 00:00:53,854
Our bodies are constantly
repairing themselves. Cells die.

18
00:00:54,324 --> 00:00:55,894
New cells replace them.

19
00:00:56,567 --> 00:00:58,197
Our skin renews itself.

20
00:00:59,007 --> 00:01:01,057
Our blood cells are constantly replaced.

21
00:01:02,087 --> 00:01:05,617
The lining of our intestines can
renew itself incredibly quickly.

22
00:01:06,502 --> 00:01:09,352
So why can't the whole body
just keep doing this forever?

23
00:01:10,222 --> 00:01:12,492
Because the repair process isn't perfect.

24
00:01:13,363 --> 00:01:14,963
DNA gets damaged.

25
00:01:15,043 --> 00:01:16,783
Mitochondria wear out.

26
00:01:16,863 --> 00:01:19,993
Waste products accumulate inside cells.

27
00:01:20,714 --> 00:01:23,524
Some cells stop working
properly, but don't die.

28
00:01:24,334 --> 00:01:25,984
These are called senescent cells.

29
00:01:26,913 --> 00:01:31,083
And over time, cells also change the
way they switch genes on and off.

30
00:01:31,953 --> 00:01:33,783
These are epigenetic changes.

31
00:01:34,713 --> 00:01:38,103
It's a little like an operating system
that has been running for decades.

32
00:01:38,613 --> 00:01:42,483
It still works. But over
time, bugs accumulate.

33
00:01:43,429 --> 00:01:45,319
Old configurations remain.

34
00:01:45,509 --> 00:01:47,139
Files become corrupted.

35
00:01:47,469 --> 00:01:49,959
And the system becomes less reliable.

36
00:01:50,823 --> 00:01:52,293
Our bodies aren't computers.

37
00:01:53,143 --> 00:01:55,373
But the analogy is not completely wrong.

38
00:01:57,783 --> 00:02:00,593
Now imagine that science
gets really good at this.

39
00:02:01,343 --> 00:02:03,193
We can remove senescent cells.

40
00:02:03,363 --> 00:02:04,853
Repair damaged DNA.

41
00:02:05,796 --> 00:02:07,556
Rejuvenate mitochondria.

42
00:02:07,636 --> 00:02:09,586
Restore stem cells.

43
00:02:10,216 --> 00:02:13,666
And reset some of the epigenetic
changes associated with ageing.

44
00:02:14,833 --> 00:02:17,763
We wouldn't just be treating
individual diseases anymore.

45
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:21,403
We could potentially keep
the body in a younger state.

46
00:02:22,205 --> 00:02:25,285
Instead of letting the body
deteriorate for eighty years

47
00:02:25,365 --> 00:02:28,735
and then trying to repair it,
we'd maintain it continuously.

48
00:02:29,853 --> 00:02:31,343
Like servicing a machine.

49
00:02:31,633 --> 00:02:34,153
Replace worn-out parts. Repair damage.

50
00:02:34,233 --> 00:02:35,603
Keep everything working.

51
00:02:36,233 --> 00:02:38,623
So why couldn't we do the
same thing with a human?

52
00:02:41,223 --> 00:02:42,793
Maybe a body is a machine.

53
00:02:43,303 --> 00:02:46,113
But not every part of a
machine can simply be swapped.

54
00:02:46,903 --> 00:02:49,693
Some are easy to replace. Others are not.

55
00:02:50,475 --> 00:02:52,585
And the most difficult one is the brain.

56
00:02:53,515 --> 00:02:56,865
The human brain contains roughly
eighty-six billion neurons.

57
00:02:57,891 --> 00:03:01,521
And those neurons are connected by
an enormous network of synapses.

58
00:03:02,571 --> 00:03:05,681
That network contains a large
part of what makes you you.

59
00:03:06,330 --> 00:03:09,060
Your memories. Your
skills. Your personality.

60
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:11,800
Everything you've
learned during your life.

61
00:03:12,593 --> 00:03:14,953
So we can't simply say:
"This neuron is old.

62
00:03:15,033 --> 00:03:19,803
Let's replace it." With a skin
cell, that's relatively simple.

63
00:03:20,513 --> 00:03:22,563
With a neuron, it could
be a very big deal.

64
00:03:23,317 --> 00:03:26,627
Because the new neuron might not
have exactly the same connections.

65
00:03:27,637 --> 00:03:29,987
And if you change enough
neurons and synapses,

66
00:03:30,217 --> 00:03:32,507
you may change the structure
of the brain itself.

67
00:03:35,267 --> 00:03:38,057
Most neurons in our brains
stay with us for decades.

68
00:03:39,007 --> 00:03:41,617
And while they're there,
damage slowly accumulates.

69
00:03:41,867 --> 00:03:44,397
Including mutations in their DNA.

70
00:03:45,415 --> 00:03:48,865
Our cells have remarkably good
systems for repairing DNA.

71
00:03:49,215 --> 00:03:51,025
But they're not perfect.

72
00:03:51,913 --> 00:03:56,053
A cell that survives for a hundred or
a hundred and fifty years has a lot

73
00:03:56,133 --> 00:04:00,783
more time to accumulate mistakes than
one that survives for only a few months.

74
00:04:01,631 --> 00:04:05,001
And eventually, enough damage
could affect how the cell works.

75
00:04:05,651 --> 00:04:07,761
And this is where that number comes in.

76
00:04:10,251 --> 00:04:14,301
A recent mathematical model
asked a fascinating question.

77
00:04:14,931 --> 00:04:18,661
What happens if we remove most
of the ordinary causes of ageing?

78
00:04:19,586 --> 00:04:21,296
The answer was surprising.

79
00:04:22,246 --> 00:04:24,546
The model estimated a
median human lifespan

80
00:04:24,626 --> 00:04:27,336
of roughly a hundred and fifty-six years.

81
00:04:28,153 --> 00:04:29,723
Median, not maximum.

82
00:04:30,133 --> 00:04:32,533
In the model, half of the population would

83
00:04:32,613 --> 00:04:35,123
reach beyond a hundred
and fifty-six years.

84
00:04:35,910 --> 00:04:37,690
But this does not mean
there is a biological

85
00:04:37,770 --> 00:04:41,630
timer that kills us at
a hundred and fifty-six.

86
00:04:41,710 --> 00:04:44,520
And it doesn't prove that
humans can't live longer.

87
00:04:45,307 --> 00:04:48,757
It's a mathematical model based on
a particular set of assumptions.

88
00:04:51,467 --> 00:04:54,297
But what happens when we
fix almost everything else?

89
00:04:55,105 --> 00:04:57,655
Some cells are extremely
difficult to replace

90
00:04:58,025 --> 00:05:00,275
without changing the
information they contain.

91
00:05:01,238 --> 00:05:04,468
The model points at two of
them. The heart. And the brain.

92
00:05:05,387 --> 00:05:06,697
A heart is a pump.

93
00:05:07,547 --> 00:05:11,117
If we ever learn to grow one, we could
simply fit a new one and lose nothing.

94
00:05:11,885 --> 00:05:13,855
We have already done a version of this.

95
00:05:14,625 --> 00:05:16,985
People have lived with an
artificial heart that has

96
00:05:17,065 --> 00:05:20,635
no pulse at all, its speed
set from outside the body.

97
00:05:21,327 --> 00:05:23,617
Not for long. Weeks, sometimes months.

98
00:05:23,767 --> 00:05:25,467
And not without complications.

99
00:05:25,547 --> 00:05:27,317
But the principle held.

100
00:05:27,747 --> 00:05:30,317
Their blood kept moving, and
they were still themselves.

101
00:05:31,518 --> 00:05:35,928
The brain is the one place where replacing
the part means replacing the information.

102
00:05:39,418 --> 00:05:42,028
Let's imagine that
science gets even better.

103
00:05:42,858 --> 00:05:45,388
We can repair the DNA inside every neuron.

104
00:05:45,518 --> 00:05:47,408
Remove damaged proteins.

105
00:05:47,638 --> 00:05:49,248
Restore mitochondria.

106
00:05:49,948 --> 00:05:52,238
Fix every problem we know how to fix.

107
00:05:53,148 --> 00:05:55,838
Maybe we could keep the
brain biologically young too.

108
00:05:56,574 --> 00:05:58,924
But that leads to a
much stranger question.

109
00:05:59,854 --> 00:06:02,004
What does it actually
mean to repair a brain?

110
00:06:02,923 --> 00:06:04,313
Think about a computer.

111
00:06:05,223 --> 00:06:08,773
If you replace a damaged
transistor, the data stays intact.

112
00:06:09,698 --> 00:06:14,148
Replace the hard drive with an exact
copy, and the data is still there.

113
00:06:14,871 --> 00:06:17,881
But what happens if you slowly
replace every component?

114
00:06:18,691 --> 00:06:21,601
At some point, none of the
original hardware remains.

115
00:06:22,591 --> 00:06:24,201
Is it still the same computer?

116
00:06:25,071 --> 00:06:26,601
And what about a human being?

117
00:06:29,061 --> 00:06:30,831
Let's take this to an extreme.

118
00:06:31,741 --> 00:06:35,511
Imagine replacing a different one
percent of your neurons every year.

119
00:06:36,242 --> 00:06:40,552
The new neurons are perfect copies.
Same function. Same connections.

120
00:06:40,702 --> 00:06:41,902
Same information.

121
00:06:42,589 --> 00:06:46,559
After one hundred years, none of
your original neurons would remain.

122
00:06:47,309 --> 00:06:50,799
But the process happened so
gradually that you never noticed.

123
00:06:51,732 --> 00:06:53,562
Would you still be the same person?

124
00:06:54,332 --> 00:06:56,342
Most people would probably say yes.

125
00:06:57,163 --> 00:06:59,773
There was never a moment when
you suddenly disappeared.

126
00:07:00,758 --> 00:07:02,588
Now change the thought experiment.

127
00:07:03,418 --> 00:07:06,218
Do exactly the same
thing... but in one second.

128
00:07:06,298 --> 00:07:07,908
Is the result different?

129
00:07:10,598 --> 00:07:13,828
And what if we could create a
perfect copy of your entire brain?

130
00:07:14,578 --> 00:07:18,528
It would have your memories. Your
personality. Your experiences.

131
00:07:19,613 --> 00:07:21,313
And it would look at you and say: "I'm

132
00:07:21,393 --> 00:07:24,783
still me." Would that
copy actually be you?

133
00:07:25,373 --> 00:07:28,763
Or would it simply be a new person
who happens to have all your memories?

134
00:07:29,676 --> 00:07:32,506
At this point, we're no longer
talking only about biology.

135
00:07:33,596 --> 00:07:35,686
We're talking about personal identity.

136
00:07:38,486 --> 00:07:40,966
And that's why I think
extending healthspan is much

137
00:07:41,046 --> 00:07:43,356
more interesting than
simply extending lifespan.

138
00:07:44,747 --> 00:07:47,077
We don't need to live
for a thousand years.

139
00:07:47,867 --> 00:07:49,417
We don't even need to
live for two hundred.

140
00:07:50,669 --> 00:07:53,389
Imagine something much
simpler. You reach forty.

141
00:07:53,469 --> 00:07:56,899
You're still physically capable.
Then fifty. Still healthy.

142
00:07:57,895 --> 00:07:59,415
Sixty. Still active. Seventy.

143
00:07:59,515 --> 00:08:01,685
Still independent. Eighty.

144
00:08:02,075 --> 00:08:04,605
Still capable of living your own life.

145
00:08:05,370 --> 00:08:09,000
What if we could slow biological
ageing enough to make that normal?

146
00:08:09,830 --> 00:08:13,340
That could matter far more than
simply pushing human lifespan higher.

147
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,210
Because age gives us something valuable.

148
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:21,510
Something we don't have
at twenty. Experience.

149
00:08:22,519 --> 00:08:25,289
A twenty-year-old may
have plenty of energy.

150
00:08:26,099 --> 00:08:28,509
But they are still working
out what to do with it.

151
00:08:29,209 --> 00:08:32,419
At forty, you've accumulated
twenty years of experience.

152
00:08:32,609 --> 00:08:34,709
At sixty, perhaps forty.

153
00:08:34,789 --> 00:08:37,999
And that experience can
be incredibly valuable.

154
00:08:38,950 --> 00:08:41,120
You may understand your profession better.

155
00:08:41,910 --> 00:08:43,800
You may understand people better.

156
00:08:44,450 --> 00:08:46,340
You may understand yourself better.

157
00:08:46,970 --> 00:08:48,860
You may simply make better decisions.

158
00:08:49,889 --> 00:08:53,699
But that's exactly when the biological
hardware starts slowing down.

159
00:08:54,389 --> 00:08:56,259
If we could keep the hardware working...

160
00:08:56,529 --> 00:08:58,179
we could keep the experience.

161
00:09:00,959 --> 00:09:04,049
Modern life doesn't always fit
very well with human biology.

162
00:09:05,339 --> 00:09:09,869
We often spend decades learning, building
careers, and figuring out what we want.

163
00:09:10,657 --> 00:09:14,807
And by the time we're ready, biology
may already be running out of time.

164
00:09:15,553 --> 00:09:20,323
Slowing biological ageing could give us
something more important than extra years.

165
00:09:21,173 --> 00:09:23,683
We'd gain freedom over
the order of our lives.

166
00:09:26,493 --> 00:09:28,803
So how long could we actually keep living?

167
00:09:29,523 --> 00:09:32,863
Maybe the future isn't a world
where humans become immortal.

168
00:09:33,738 --> 00:09:35,568
Maybe it's something more interesting.

169
00:09:36,618 --> 00:09:38,638
A world where the
boundaries between youth,

170
00:09:38,718 --> 00:09:41,068
middle age and old age start to blur.

171
00:09:41,921 --> 00:09:46,671
A hundred-year-old person might not
resemble today's hundred-year-old at all.

172
00:09:47,351 --> 00:09:51,011
And if we eventually learn how
to repair the brain itself, even

173
00:09:51,091 --> 00:09:54,641
a hundred and fifty-six years
might not be a meaningful limit.

174
00:09:55,404 --> 00:09:58,534
Because that number depends on
what we believe we can repair.

175
00:09:59,468 --> 00:10:01,578
And technology has a strange habit.

176
00:10:02,348 --> 00:10:06,398
When we don't know how to fix something,
we tend to think it's a fundamental limit.

177
00:10:06,828 --> 00:10:09,638
Until someone figures out how to fix it.

178
00:10:12,168 --> 00:10:16,338
So maybe the real question isn't:
"How long can a human life last?"

179
00:10:19,128 --> 00:10:22,018
Maybe it's: "How long can we
keep a human being human?"

180
00:10:24,988 --> 00:10:28,398
And that may be a much more
interesting question than immortality.
